1. App Server and Decoupling Code & Storage
When you access a website, your browser first contacts the DNS to find the website’s IP address.

Scale requires distributing requests across many machines—load balancers and sharding (via consistent hashing) become necessary.

Old Model: Both code and storage on the same machine.

Problems:

Tight coupling makes code deployments harder (can cause downtime).

Fewer resources for the code, as storage also shares resources.

Ideal Model: Decouple code and storage for efficiency, result in addition of latency but better scaling.

App Servers: Machines that store only code (stateless, easily scalable).

2. What is Caching?
Caching means storing things in a faster-access memory/location, so data can be fetched quickly.

Caching can occur at different levels:

Browser (In-Browser) Caching:

Stores DNS results, static images/scripts locally for faster access on repeat visits.

CDN (Content Delivery Network):

Why? To deliver images/media from nearby servers for users across the globe.

Examples: Akamai, Cloudflare, AWS CloudFront, Fastly.

CDN ensures fast delivery by having user requests handled by the nearest server.

Techniques: Integration with ISPs, Anycast routing.

Local Caching:

Data cached on application servers to reduce database hits.

Global Caching: (To be discussed in the next class)

E.g. Redis, Memcache for fast fetching of data in memory.

3. Problems Related to Caching
Cache is limited in size

Cache is not the actual data source

Staleness: Data in cache may become outdated if the database source changes.

Full cache: Not enough room for all data, so some has to be removed.

4. Solutions & Strategies
A. Cache Invalidation
TTL (Time to Live):

Each cache entry is valid for only a fixed period.

After expiry, data is removed and fetched again from the main source.

Keeping Cache & DB in Sync

Write-through cache: Data written to cache first, then to the database. Good for read-heavy systems.

Write-back cache: Data written to cache and success returned; database is updated asynchronously later. Good for analytics but not for critical data.

Write-around cache: Writes bypass cache, go directly to the database. Cache is synced via TTL or similar strategies.

B. Cache Eviction
When cache is full, old data needs removal.

Eviction Strategies:

FIFO (First In, First Out)

LRU (Least Recently Used)

LIFO (Last In, First Out)

MRU (Most Recently Used)

Choose the best strategy based on which data is most likely to be accessed again (aim for high cache hit rate).

